  "result": "Fixed. The command now prints **27.06**.\n\n**Root cause:** `to_cents` in `shop/money.py:9` padded the decimal part on the wrong side. `\"5\".rjust(2, \"0\")` gives `\"05\"`, so `\"12.5\"` became 1205 cents instead of 1250. Two of those plus 8.25% tax printed 26.09. I changed it to `frac.ljust(2, \"0\")`, which gives `\"50\"`, so `\"12.5\"` is now 1250 cents and 2500 plus tax is 27.06. I also dropped the `or 0`, since `ljust` already turns an empty decimal part into `\"00\"`.\n\n**Tests:** pytest isn't installed, so I ran them with `python3 -m unittest`, and all 6 pass. None of them call `to_cents` with a one-digit decimal, which is why the bug got through. You may want a test like `to_cents(\"12.5\") == 1250`.\n\n**Separate bug I left alone:** negative amounts are also converted wrongly. `to_cents(\"-12.5\")` gives -1150 instead of -1250, because the decimal part is added to the whole number without its sign. Tell me if you want that fixed too.",
